00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
let's bring in now retired air force  general richard myers former chairman of  the joint chiefs of staff under  president george w bush general myers  thanks for being here  you bet hard to believe 20 years you  know back then uh you had another three  weeks before you were permanently going  to be the chairman of the joint chiefs  talk to me about the reflections of 9 11  that day  well i was acting chairman that day as i  think you know and  it was a day that's  imprinted on my memory will never leave  of course  the visuals of people  leaping out of the world trade centers  to save themselves  uh the carnage the loved ones  lost and the loved ones left behind uh  the heroic acts by first responders  uh fire police medical  and of course uh the members on uh the  group the the pastors on flight 93 that  crashed in pennsylvania so that's i  think a lot about that i also think  about how unified this country was  uh  right after 9 11 driving around  washington dc and seeing cars with  american flags  uh streaming out of them all over the  city uh by everybody uh we were we were  very  cohesive in that day in our in our  thoughts and  and our wishes for  you know dealing with al qaeda i can  remember the visits to congress a lot of  solidarity there as well of course 20  years a lot has changed for a lot of  reasons  but uh i kind of long for that  solidarity that that 911 brought to the  united states of america you wish you  had done something different after 9 11  you've had kind of two decades to look  back  and uh from your perspective your  vantage point  yeah so for my my perch i think the one  thing that frustrated me the longer i  was  in office and of course i left in 2005  but  was the inability of the united states  to bring all our  instruments of national power  to bear on the problem  so uh  i think we overused a military  instrument and i mean just calling it a  war on terror if it's a war that must  mean the military right but it was more  than that uh it was competition a battle  of ideas  uh  competition inside the uh  islam to figure their way to deal with  modernity and these are issues uh left  to the economic  uh instruments of power informational  political  um diplomatic and i don't think those  were brought to bear in the way they  they should have been  uh to to help with the military peace  which the military is going to play a  piece of this of course a big role but  but they can't do it alone and i that's  one of the things that is  that has frustrated me that we could  never kind of get that getting traction  on those kind of ideas i wanted to play  a sound by back from the press  conference where we just initiated  military action inside afghanistan take  a listen  today our forces have begun the initial  part  of military operations in the war  against terrorism visible or not  our friends and enemies should  understand that all instruments of our  national power  as well as those of our friends and  allies around the world  are being brought to bear on this global  menace innocent lives are still at risk  today and will be  until we have dealt with the terrorists  if there were an easy  safe way to route terrorist networks  out of countries that are harboring them  it would be a blessing  but there is not  now it's interesting to look back at the  start of that and today general the the  taliban that you called a terror group  now  is in control of afghanistan just get  your thoughts on how things have gone  over the last couple of weeks and what  you've seen  well it's  i mean for for those of us that  we're on the front end of this uh that  saw the the hope  and the eyes and thoughts and  conversations we had with our afghan  friends  uh to have that all  dashed or at least  most of it dashed at this point  is is just  just so sad  and  you know of course the way it all ended  was a little bit  chaotic a lot chaotic it would have been  nice if it could have been more orderly  but  i'm  i don't have any inside information i  don't know about all the planning that  went behind all that it was just kind of  sad to see it  come out like that and then um since  we've been so involved in afghan  charities and so forth since  uh my 10 years particularly my wife mary  jo  lots of calls for help to get us on list  that would get  people that felt they were at risk or  their families were at risk to get them  out of afghanistan and it's just  heartbreaking i mean  you know 20 years  a lot of people have been educated a lot  of women have been educated in those 20  years thousands of people have had  a  a better education than they ever had  hoped for under the tata bomb before him  and so where that where is that going to  go  i don't think any of us know at this  point we'll see if this taliban is a new  and better taliban i think a lot of us  have our doubts but we're hopeful too  that  uh maybe they'll maybe they'll be  different this time around which is  enough different to give everybody a  chance yeah we just heard from lucas  thomason down at guantanamo bay  why do you think there hasn't been a  trial for the 911 mastermind we're at 20  years here and we're pre-trial  hearings  yeah i think  you know it's it's well documented that  uh  several presidents have tried really  hard to close it down and expedite these  trials and the complexities of the  issues involved  uh are such i guess that they just can't  get it done i do not know the  intricacies anymore uh when i was on  active duty we were not quite to the  trial stage when i was still in service  and i so i haven't fought it closely  enough to know that the legal  complexities of why this isn't happening  but it's um it's not it's clearly not  good it's not good for the us image  um as bad as these people are they need  a trial that's one of the things that we  guarantee our citizens i know they're  not our citizens but uh the right thing  to do is get on with the trial and let  justice take its course two more things  should the military be held accountable  for  let's say happy talk about the status of  afghan national forces considering what  we saw  uh in recent days  is the media involved in that should we  have been asking tougher questions to  you all  you know that's a great that's a great  question and i'm i  those were the same questions we had  when i was chairman as we're starting to  train people and how many resources have  you put to training afghan forces and so  forth can they  can they be  an effective force in the end it it  turned out they weren't but  i think what they rely on a lot  as the country was trying to become a  country and not a  a tribal a group of tribes  i think they relied heavily on u.s  advisers to  to focus them  and to to be the informal leaders and  the inspiration for what they were doing  and without that then it falls apart  pretty quickly and i'm also told there  was a lot of corruption in the hierarchy  in the afghan army so  uh the high ranking officers were maybe  not always paying the lower ranking  officers which doesn't build a lot of  loyalty obviously so there's  i think there were a lot of issues with  that and i think we all should have been  asking a lot of questions i you know i  obviously i haven't been in  many briefing rooms where that was  discussed so i i can't give you the  specifics on  last thing it's a it's a heavy one for  the last one in retrospect in 20 years  looking back do you think going into  iraq was a mistake and  drained resources and attention from  afghanistan  yeah i don't  personally i i don't think that was  as big an issue as people like to make  it certainly  those are two big operations they do  take i mean it's the intellectual  uh capital and the energy you have to  deal with with problems  but  you know i think we we could handle that  things ebbed and flowed right so  afghanistan at one point was looking  pretty good then it got worse and it got  a little bit better and  